If `params.payload.text` is an object (like the `ReplyPayload` object itself), then `resolveInteractiveTextFallback` would receive `text` as an object. Let me check what that function does:

```typescript
export function resolveInteractiveTextFallback(params: {
  text?: string;
  interactive?: InteractiveReply;
}): string | undefined {
  const text = readTrimmedString(params.text);
  if (text) {
    return params.text;
  }
  ...
}
```

The issue is that `readTrimmedString` might not handle objects properly. If `params.text` is an object, `readTrimmedString` would return undefined, and the function would fall through to the interactive blocks check, which wouldn't help either. This means `text` would end up as an object instead of a string, which is exactly what we're seeing.

Then when `sendPayloadMediaSequenceOrFallback` is called with this malformed `text` value, it would try to use an object where a string is expected, causing the `[object Object]` output.

