Now I see the bug clearly! In `memory-flush.ts` at line 146-149:```typescript
if (text.includes(SILENT_REPLY_TOKEN)) {
  // ...
}return `${text}\n\nIf no user-visible reply is needed, start with ${SILENT_REPLY_TOKEN}.`;
```

The prompt tells the agent to START with `NO_REPLY`, but the `isSilentReplyText` function checks for EXACT match (with optional surrounding whitespace):```typescript
const regex = new RegExp(`^\\s*${escaped}\\s*$`);
```

So if the agent says "NO_REPLY" at the START of the message, `isSilentReplyText` will match it. But the issue is that `SILENT_REPLY_TOKEN` is `"NO_REPLY"` (not `"[[NO_REPLY]]"`), so the agent is told to say "NO_REPLY" (the exact string).


But wait—if the agent responds with `NO_REPLY`, it should trigger the silent reply logic. Let me trace through what actually happens when the agent says that. The pi-agent-core creates a message with content blocks, and when the `NO_REPLY` text gets processed, it should be recognized as a silent reply and filtered out before reaching the channel.

Actually, I think I see the real issue now. Looking at how the pi-agent handles the message content, when there's a text block with "NO_REPLY", it gets wrapped in some structure that might not be getting parsed correctly downstream. Let me check if the content is being properly unwrapped when it reaches the Telegram channel.

The most direct approach would be to add type checking in `normalizeReplyPayloadDirectives` to ensure `text` is actually a string before processing it, and then trace through where the `[object Object]` string is coming from in the pi-agent-core message handling.

